 actions\nL153: ## Summary\nL154: \nL155: While characterizing ICICLE v4.0.0's BabyBear NTT on a consumer Blackwell GPU (RTX 5070, sm_120), I found that `reorder_digits_and_normalize` is the dominant cost of the forward NTT and leaves significant bandwidth on the table, while the butterfly kernels are already near the memory ceiling. A focused prototype suggests ~2× is recoverable on that pass via a coalesced (shared-memory-staged / COBRA-style) permutation.\nL156: Sharing in case it's useful. Full write-up, profiles, and reproducible harnesses: 【72†https://github.com/pscamillo/icicle-blackwell-ntt】\nL157: ## Measurements (RTX 5070, 2^22, BabyBear, mixed-radix forward NTT)\nL158: \nL159: Per-kernel Nsight Compute:\nL160: \nL161:   * `ntt64` — ~25% of NTT kernel time, 84.7% of DRAM peak — at the memory ceiling.\nL162:   * `ntt32dit` — ~28%, 76.5% of DRAM peak — near the ceiling.\nL163:   * `reorder_digits_and_normalize` — ~39% of NTT kernel time, 46.2% of DRAM peak, with global-store efficiency of 4 of 32 bytes per sector (12.5%) across 99.8% of sectors — the scatter signature of the digit-reversal permutation.\nL164: ## Recoverable headroom (prototype, same GPU / size / dtype)\nL165: \nL166: kernel  | global-store bytes/sector  | throughput\nL167: --- | --- | ---\nL168: coalesced copy (ceiling)  | 32 / 32  | ~1320 GB/s\nL169: scattered bit-reversal (= reorder pattern; same 4/32 signature `ncu` flags)  | 4 / 32  | ~375 GB/s\nL170: shared-memory tiled transpose (coalesced permutation)  | 32 / 32  | ~780 GB/s (~2.1×)\nL171: Since the reorder is ~39% of NTT kernel time, halving it maps to an estimated ~15–20% end-to-end NTT speedup. The transpose is just the coalescing primitive; a drop-in would be a COBRA-style digit-reversal (tiled staging + index reversal).\nL172: ## Environment\nL173: \nL174: ICICLE v4.0.0 (babybear frontend built from source; CUDA backend `ubuntu22-cuda122`), CUDA 12.9, driver 575.57.08, RTX 5070 (sm_120), Ubuntu 24.04. Single machine; all numbers and harnesses are in the linked repo.\nL175: ## Questions\nL176: \nL177:   1. Would a coalesced reimplementation of the digit-reversal/reorder pass be welcome, or is it already being addressed in newer work?\nL178:   2. (Minor, separate) The shipped v4.0.0 CUDA backend has no sm_120 cubin — it runs on Blackwell via `compute_89` PTX-JIT. Are official sm_120 / sm_100 binaries planned?\nL179: \nL180: Happy to share more detail or test patches on Blackwell.\nL181: \nL182: Reactions are currently unavailable\nL183: ## Activity\nL184: \nL185:   1. ### uulong950 commented on Jul 2, 2026\nL186: \nL187: 【73†uulong950】\nL188: \nL189: 【74†on Jul 2, 2026】\nL190: \nL191: More actions\nL192: \nL193: This is a very interesting profile. The `reorder_digits_and_normalize` result matches what we have seen from the Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE side: once the butterfly kernels are close to the memory ceiling, the end-to-end NTT performance is often dominated by digit reversal, layout conversion, transpose, and permutation contracts rather than field arithmetic alone.\nL194: In qingming-g64-ntt, we ended up treating the output layout contract, radix decomposition, transpose, and twiddle application as one fused design problem, instead of optimizing butterfly kernels in isolation. The current AMD implementation is not directly comparable to this ICICLE BabyBear/Blackwell profile, but the architectural lesson seems aligned: coalesced permutation and layout-aware NTT design can matter as much as arithmetic throughput.\nL195: I will open a separate issue to discuss whether a Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE benchmark contract would be useful for ICICLE, so this BabyBear reorder issue can stay focused.\nL196: \nL197:   2. ### pscamillo commented on Jul 2, 2026\nL198: \nL199: 【70†pscamillo】\nL200: \nL201: 【75†on Jul 2, 2026】\nL202: \nL203: Author\nL204: \nL205: More actions\nL206: \nL207: Thanks for the confirmation — good to know this lines up with the Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE side.\nL208: That convergence is the part I found most reassuring on the Blackwell profile: the butterflies (`ntt64`, `ntt32dit`) are already sitting at ~77–85% of DRAM peak, so there's essentially no arithmetic headroom left, and everything recoverable lives in the reorder/permutation contract.\nL209: The `reorder_digits_and_normalize` pass is a pure scatter (4/32 bytes per sector across ~99.8% of sectors), so it reads as a layout/coalescing problem rather than a compute one — which is exactly the \"treat layout, radix decomposition, transpose and twiddle as one fused design problem\" framing you describe.\nL210: Splitting the STARK-LDE benchmark contract into 【76†#1047】 makes sense to me — happy to keep this one focused on the BabyBear reorder. I'll follow 【76†#1047】.\nL211: \nL212: 🎉React with 🎉1 Reacted by uulong950\nL213: \nL214: to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? 【77†Sign in to comment】\nL215: ## Metadata\nL216: \nL217: ## Metadata\nL218: \nL219: ### Assignees\nL220: \nL221: No one assigned\nL222: \nL223: ### Labels\nL224: \nL225: No labels\nL226: \nL227: No labels\nL228: \nL229: ### Type\nL230: \nL231: No type\nL232: \nL233: ### Projects\nL234: \nL235: No projects\nL236: \nL237: ### Milestone\nL238: \nL239: No milestone\nL240: \nL241: ### Relationships\nL242: \nL243: None yet\nL244: \nL245: ### Development\nL246: \nL247: No branches or pull requests\nL248: \nL249: ## Issue actions\nL250: \nL251:   * 【78†Image†github.githubassets.com】Open in GitHub Copilot app\nL252: \nL253: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n"
